1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,313
On a cold morning,

2
00:00:01,313 --> 00:00:03,867
before takeoff, a truck with a long

3
00:00:03,867 --> 00:00:06,348
arm drives up to your airplane and

4
00:00:06,348 --> 00:00:08,901
sprays it with a hot orange liquid,

5
00:00:08,901 --> 00:00:11,528
and then sometimes with a green one.

6
00:00:11,708 --> 00:00:13,508
It looks like a car wash.

7
00:00:13,688 --> 00:00:15,937
It is actually one of the most

8
00:00:15,937 --> 00:00:18,937
important safety steps in winter flying,

9
00:00:18,937 --> 00:00:21,711
because a layer of frost thinner than

10
00:00:21,711 --> 00:00:24,636
sandpaper can stop a wing from lifting.

11
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:28,631
Today, ten parts that keep airplanes free

12
00:00:28,631 --> 00:00:31,608
of ice, and why each one exists.

13
00:00:31,958 --> 00:00:34,626
Ice is one of the oldest enemies

14
00:00:34,626 --> 00:00:35,626
of aviation.

15
00:00:35,806 --> 00:00:38,508
It changes the shape of the wing,

16
00:00:38,508 --> 00:00:41,864
blocks sensors, and adds weight in places

17
00:00:41,864 --> 00:00:43,910
where it does not belong.

18
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:45,457
Over the decades,

19
00:00:45,457 --> 00:00:49,075
airplanes and airports have collected a whole

20
00:00:49,075 --> 00:00:51,005
set of tools against it,

21
00:00:51,005 --> 00:00:52,533
some on the ground,

22
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:54,865
some built into the airplane,

23
00:00:54,865 --> 00:00:57,518
and many of them were added after

24
00:00:57,518 --> 00:00:58,161
a crash.

25
00:00:58,512 --> 00:01:01,500
As always, every fact here comes from

26
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:02,711
a named source,

27
00:01:02,711 --> 00:01:05,618
the Federal Aviation Administration,

28
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:08,929
accident reports and aviation references,

29
00:01:08,929 --> 00:01:11,352
all listed in the description.

30
00:01:11,532 --> 00:01:13,970
Let's start with the orange liquid.

31
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:17,270
Why do airports spray airplanes with hot

32
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:18,303
orange liquid?

33
00:01:18,483 --> 00:01:20,064
It is de-icing fluid,

34
00:01:20,064 --> 00:01:22,398
mostly glycol mixed with water,

35
00:01:22,398 --> 00:01:25,034
the same family of chemicals as the

36
00:01:25,034 --> 00:01:26,540
antifreeze in a car.

37
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:28,135
It is sprayed hot,

38
00:01:28,135 --> 00:01:32,222
between about fifty-five and eighty degrees Celsius,

39
00:01:32,222 --> 00:01:33,558
at high pressure.

40
00:01:33,738 --> 00:01:36,125
The heat melts the snow and ice,

41
00:01:36,125 --> 00:01:38,139
the pressure blasts it off,

42
00:01:38,139 --> 00:01:40,899
and the glycol stops it from freezing

43
00:01:40,899 --> 00:01:42,690
again for a few minutes.

44
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,489
This is called Type one fluid,

45
00:01:45,489 --> 00:01:48,183
and it is dyed orange on purpose.

46
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:51,471
Glycol and water are almost invisible on

47
00:01:51,471 --> 00:01:52,792
a white airplane,

48
00:01:52,792 --> 00:01:55,045
so the color lets the crew on

49
00:01:55,045 --> 00:01:57,842
the truck see exactly which parts of

50
00:01:57,842 --> 00:02:00,174
the wing and tail are covered,

51
00:02:00,174 --> 00:02:02,271
and which they have missed.

52
00:02:02,451 --> 00:02:05,301
A big airliner can need hundreds of

53
00:02:05,301 --> 00:02:08,801
gallons, often between five hundred and one

54
00:02:08,801 --> 00:02:09,534
thousand.

55
00:02:09,883 --> 00:02:12,333
Orange fluid has one weakness.

56
00:02:12,513 --> 00:02:13,376
It is thin,

57
00:02:13,376 --> 00:02:16,043
and it flows off the wing quickly.

58
00:02:16,223 --> 00:02:18,060
It cleans the airplane,

59
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:20,456
but it does not protect it for

60
00:02:20,456 --> 00:02:20,855
long.

61
00:02:21,035 --> 00:02:22,815
If snow keeps falling,

62
00:02:22,815 --> 00:02:25,243
the wing can be dirty again in

63
00:02:25,243 --> 00:02:26,376
a few minutes.

64
00:02:26,556 --> 00:02:27,471
That is why,

65
00:02:27,471 --> 00:02:28,539
in heavy snow,

66
00:02:28,539 --> 00:02:30,141
a second truck comes,

67
00:02:30,141 --> 00:02:31,896
with a different color.

68
00:02:32,246 --> 00:02:34,851
Why do some airplanes get sprayed with

69
00:02:34,851 --> 00:02:37,181
green liquid after the orange one?

70
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:40,855
Green means Type four anti-icing fluid.

71
00:02:41,035 --> 00:02:41,945
It is thick,

72
00:02:41,945 --> 00:02:42,779
like a gel,

73
00:02:42,779 --> 00:02:44,826
and it stays on the wing as

74
00:02:44,826 --> 00:02:46,190
a protective coat.

75
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:48,690
Falling snow lands on it and melts

76
00:02:48,690 --> 00:02:51,010
into it instead of freezing to the

77
00:02:51,010 --> 00:02:51,351
wing.

78
00:02:51,531 --> 00:02:53,330
Depending on the weather,

79
00:02:53,330 --> 00:02:55,922
it can protect the airplane for much

80
00:02:55,922 --> 00:02:58,009
longer than the orange fluid.

81
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:00,552
The clever part is that the gel

82
00:03:00,552 --> 00:03:02,816
is designed to stay on while the

83
00:03:02,816 --> 00:03:04,726
airplane is standing still,

84
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:07,202
and to slide off during the takeoff

85
00:03:07,202 --> 00:03:07,485
run.

86
00:03:07,665 --> 00:03:09,817
As the airplane accelerates,

87
00:03:09,817 --> 00:03:12,738
the airflow shears the thick fluid off

88
00:03:12,738 --> 00:03:14,737
the wing, so it is gone by

89
00:03:14,737 --> 00:03:17,197
the time the wing needs to lift.

90
00:03:17,377 --> 00:03:20,580
It is called a pseudoplastic fluid,

91
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:22,136
thick when still,

92
00:03:22,136 --> 00:03:23,693
thin when moving.

93
00:03:24,042 --> 00:03:27,221
Pilots use tables called holdover times,

94
00:03:27,221 --> 00:03:30,240
which say how long each fluid protects

95
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:33,180
the airplane in each kind of weather.

96
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,193
For the thin orange fluid,

97
00:03:35,193 --> 00:03:37,237
it can be just a few minutes.

98
00:03:37,417 --> 00:03:39,073
For the thick green one,

99
00:03:39,073 --> 00:03:40,937
it can be more than an hour

100
00:03:40,937 --> 00:03:42,317
in light conditions.

101
00:03:42,497 --> 00:03:45,229
If the time runs out before takeoff,

102
00:03:45,229 --> 00:03:47,657
the airplane must go back and be

103
00:03:47,657 --> 00:03:48,720
sprayed again.

104
00:03:49,069 --> 00:03:51,559
Why must a wing be perfectly clean

105
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:52,657
before takeoff?

106
00:03:52,837 --> 00:03:55,675
Because a wing works only if its

107
00:03:55,675 --> 00:03:57,005
shape is exact.

108
00:03:57,185 --> 00:03:59,495
Even a thin layer of frost or

109
00:03:59,495 --> 00:04:02,123
snow makes the top surface rough,

110
00:04:02,123 --> 00:04:05,310
the air separates from the wing earlier,

111
00:04:05,310 --> 00:04:07,858
and the wing makes less lift and

112
00:04:07,858 --> 00:04:09,770
stalls at a lower angle.

113
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:12,814
The rule is called the clean aircraft

114
00:04:12,814 --> 00:04:13,433
concept.

115
00:04:13,613 --> 00:04:16,374
No frost, ice or snow on the

116
00:04:16,374 --> 00:04:19,824
wings, tail or controls at takeoff.

117
00:04:20,174 --> 00:04:24,087
On the thirteenth of January nineteen eighty-two,

118
00:04:24,087 --> 00:04:27,201
Air Florida flight ninety took off from

119
00:04:27,201 --> 00:04:30,635
Washington National Airport in a snowstorm.

120
00:04:30,815 --> 00:04:32,465
It had been de-iced,

121
00:04:32,465 --> 00:04:34,775
but it waited a long time in

122
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:37,332
line, and snow and ice built up

123
00:04:37,332 --> 00:04:37,827
again.

124
00:04:38,007 --> 00:04:40,057
The crew also did not turn on

125
00:04:40,057 --> 00:04:42,106
the engines' anti-ice system.

126
00:04:42,286 --> 00:04:44,578
The airplane barely climbed,

127
00:04:44,578 --> 00:04:45,642
hit a bridge,

128
00:04:45,642 --> 00:04:48,834
and fell into the frozen Potomac River.

129
00:04:49,014 --> 00:04:51,011
Seventy-eight people died.

130
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:55,052
That crash and others changed winter flying.

131
00:04:55,232 --> 00:04:59,362
Holdover times, better fluids and stricter checks

132
00:04:59,362 --> 00:05:01,722
before takeoff all followed.

133
00:05:01,902 --> 00:05:04,794
Today, if pilots have any doubt about

134
00:05:04,794 --> 00:05:07,529
the wings, they go back for another

135
00:05:07,529 --> 00:05:07,998
spray.

136
00:05:08,178 --> 00:05:10,523
Being late is always better than taking

137
00:05:10,523 --> 00:05:11,846
off with a dirty wing.

138
00:05:12,196 --> 00:05:14,621
Why do many big airports have a

139
00:05:14,621 --> 00:05:17,593
special parking area right next to the

140
00:05:17,593 --> 00:05:18,141
runway?

141
00:05:18,321 --> 00:05:20,271
It is the de-icing pad.

142
00:05:20,451 --> 00:05:23,470
Instead of spraying the airplane at the

143
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:26,643
gate and then taxiing for twenty minutes,

144
00:05:26,643 --> 00:05:30,049
the airplane is sprayed just before takeoff,

145
00:05:30,049 --> 00:05:33,067
so the protection lasts until it leaves

146
00:05:33,067 --> 00:05:33,919
the ground.

147
00:05:34,099 --> 00:05:36,796
Several airplanes can be treated at the

148
00:05:36,796 --> 00:05:39,010
same time, like a drive through.

149
00:05:39,359 --> 00:05:41,854
The pads are also built for the

150
00:05:41,854 --> 00:05:42,819
environment.

151
00:05:42,999 --> 00:05:45,734
The used fluid is full of glycol,

152
00:05:45,734 --> 00:05:48,468
and when it breaks down in rivers

153
00:05:48,468 --> 00:05:51,203
and lakes, it uses up oxygen that

154
00:05:51,203 --> 00:05:52,032
fish need.

155
00:05:52,212 --> 00:05:54,864
So the pads have special drains,

156
00:05:54,864 --> 00:05:58,511
and some airports use glycol recovery trucks

157
00:05:58,511 --> 00:06:01,246
that vacuum up the used fluid and

158
00:06:01,246 --> 00:06:01,744
slush.

159
00:06:01,924 --> 00:06:04,118
Some of it can even be recycled.

160
00:06:04,467 --> 00:06:05,359
At the pad,

161
00:06:05,359 --> 00:06:08,113
the crew stays in contact by radio

162
00:06:08,113 --> 00:06:09,976
with the de-icing team,

163
00:06:09,976 --> 00:06:12,730
who tell them which fluid was used

164
00:06:12,730 --> 00:06:15,160
and when the spraying started.

165
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:18,129
That start time is when the holdover

166
00:06:18,129 --> 00:06:19,137
clock begins.

167
00:06:19,317 --> 00:06:20,568
From that moment,

168
00:06:20,568 --> 00:06:23,293
the pilots know exactly how much time

169
00:06:23,293 --> 00:06:24,913
they have to take off.

170
00:06:25,262 --> 00:06:27,037
Why are the front edges of an

171
00:06:27,037 --> 00:06:29,117
airliner's wings heated in flight?

172
00:06:29,297 --> 00:06:32,626
Because flying through clouds below freezing can

173
00:06:32,626 --> 00:06:34,706
build ice on the leading edge,

174
00:06:34,706 --> 00:06:36,994
the part that hits the air first.

175
00:06:37,174 --> 00:06:38,194
On many jets,

176
00:06:38,194 --> 00:06:40,860
hot air is taken from the engines,

177
00:06:40,860 --> 00:06:42,193
called bleed air,

178
00:06:42,193 --> 00:06:44,937
and piped through the inside of the

179
00:06:44,937 --> 00:06:47,995
leading edge, so ice cannot form there.

180
00:06:48,344 --> 00:06:50,852
Ice in flight is different from snow

181
00:06:50,852 --> 00:06:51,827
on the ground.

182
00:06:52,007 --> 00:06:54,968
Clouds can contain supercooled water,

183
00:06:54,968 --> 00:06:58,649
tiny droplets still liquid below zero degrees,

184
00:06:58,649 --> 00:07:01,690
that freeze the instant they touch the

185
00:07:01,690 --> 00:07:02,410
airplane.

186
00:07:02,590 --> 00:07:04,977
The leading edge collects most of them,

187
00:07:04,977 --> 00:07:06,874
so that is where the heat goes.

188
00:07:07,054 --> 00:07:10,380
Newer airplanes like the Boeing seven eight

189
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:13,630
seven use electric heating mats instead of

190
00:07:13,630 --> 00:07:14,403
bleed air.

191
00:07:14,753 --> 00:07:17,555
The pilots turn the wing anti-ice on

192
00:07:17,555 --> 00:07:20,202
when they fly in icing conditions,

193
00:07:20,202 --> 00:07:23,237
and many airplanes have an ice detector

194
00:07:23,237 --> 00:07:24,483
that warns them.

195
00:07:24,663 --> 00:07:26,832
Heating a wing uses energy,

196
00:07:26,832 --> 00:07:29,001
so the system is not on all

197
00:07:29,001 --> 00:07:29,725
the time.

198
00:07:29,905 --> 00:07:31,843
It is a tool for the moments

199
00:07:31,843 --> 00:07:33,850
when the air is full of water

200
00:07:33,850 --> 00:07:35,303
that wants to freeze.

201
00:07:35,653 --> 00:07:37,422
Why is the front lip of a

202
00:07:37,422 --> 00:07:38,695
jet engine heated?

203
00:07:38,875 --> 00:07:41,309
Because ice on the inlet can break

204
00:07:41,309 --> 00:07:43,814
off and be swallowed by the engine,

205
00:07:43,814 --> 00:07:45,532
damaging the fan blades,

206
00:07:45,532 --> 00:07:48,109
and ice can also disturb the airflow

207
00:07:48,109 --> 00:07:49,255
into the engine.

208
00:07:49,435 --> 00:07:51,855
Hot air from the engine itself is

209
00:07:51,855 --> 00:07:54,203
piped into the lip of the inlet,

210
00:07:54,203 --> 00:07:56,110
keeping it above freezing.

211
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:58,836
That system was part of the Air

212
00:07:58,836 --> 00:07:59,909
Florida story.

213
00:08:00,089 --> 00:08:03,355
Investigators found that the crew did not

214
00:08:03,355 --> 00:08:05,267
turn on engine anti-ice.

215
00:08:05,447 --> 00:08:07,798
Ice blocked a pressure probe in the

216
00:08:07,798 --> 00:08:09,746
engines that measures thrust,

217
00:08:09,746 --> 00:08:12,501
so the instruments showed more power than

218
00:08:12,501 --> 00:08:14,584
the engines were really making.

219
00:08:14,764 --> 00:08:17,047
The pilots took off with less thrust

220
00:08:17,047 --> 00:08:18,189
than they thought.

221
00:08:18,538 --> 00:08:22,709
Today, engine anti-ice is on many checklists

222
00:08:22,709 --> 00:08:25,458
for taxi and takeoff in cold,

223
00:08:25,458 --> 00:08:26,596
wet weather.

224
00:08:26,776 --> 00:08:29,623
A small switch on the overhead panel,

225
00:08:29,623 --> 00:08:31,778
a little hot air sent to the

226
00:08:31,778 --> 00:08:34,780
right place, and the engine can breathe

227
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:36,319
through a snowstorm.

228
00:08:36,669 --> 00:08:39,378
Why do many propeller airplanes have black

229
00:08:39,378 --> 00:08:41,635
rubber strips on the front of their

230
00:08:41,635 --> 00:08:42,022
wings?

231
00:08:42,202 --> 00:08:43,769
They are de-icing boots.

232
00:08:43,949 --> 00:08:45,457
When ice builds up,

233
00:08:45,457 --> 00:08:48,235
air is pumped into tubes inside the

234
00:08:48,235 --> 00:08:50,298
rubber, the boots inflate,

235
00:08:50,298 --> 00:08:52,759
and the ice cracks and is blown

236
00:08:52,759 --> 00:08:54,108
away by the wind.

237
00:08:54,288 --> 00:08:56,041
Then they deflate again.

238
00:08:56,221 --> 00:08:59,141
Smaller airplanes use them because they do

239
00:08:59,141 --> 00:09:01,366
not have much hot air from their

240
00:09:01,366 --> 00:09:02,548
engines to spare.

241
00:09:02,898 --> 00:09:05,360
Boots remove ice after it forms,

242
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:07,975
so they only protect the area they

243
00:09:07,975 --> 00:09:08,436
cover.

244
00:09:08,616 --> 00:09:12,929
On the thirty-first of October nineteen ninety-four,

245
00:09:12,929 --> 00:09:16,412
American Eagle flight four one eight four,

246
00:09:16,412 --> 00:09:17,987
an ATR seventy-two,

247
00:09:17,987 --> 00:09:20,143
was holding near Roselawn,

248
00:09:20,143 --> 00:09:22,548
Indiana, in freezing drizzle.

249
00:09:22,728 --> 00:09:25,122
Large drops froze into a ridge of

250
00:09:25,122 --> 00:09:26,645
ice behind the boots,

251
00:09:26,645 --> 00:09:28,603
where they could not reach.

252
00:09:28,783 --> 00:09:31,466
The airplane rolled out of control and

253
00:09:31,466 --> 00:09:34,925
crashed, killing all sixty-eight people on board.

254
00:09:35,274 --> 00:09:38,356
After Roselawn, the rules for flying in

255
00:09:38,356 --> 00:09:42,148
freezing drizzle and large drops were rewritten,

256
00:09:42,148 --> 00:09:45,309
and pilots were trained to recognize the

257
00:09:45,309 --> 00:09:47,047
signs of severe icing.

258
00:09:47,227 --> 00:09:48,922
The boots did their job.

259
00:09:49,102 --> 00:09:50,831
The problem was ice in a place

260
00:09:50,831 --> 00:09:52,330
the boots could not reach.

261
00:09:52,510 --> 00:09:56,709
In aviation, protection ends exactly where the

262
00:09:56,709 --> 00:09:58,535
protected area ends.

263
00:09:58,885 --> 00:10:00,919
Why are the small tubes on an

264
00:10:00,919 --> 00:10:02,531
airplane's nose heated?

265
00:10:02,711 --> 00:10:04,252
They are pitot tubes,

266
00:10:04,252 --> 00:10:06,159
and they measure airspeed.

267
00:10:06,339 --> 00:10:08,125
Air rams into the hole at the

268
00:10:08,125 --> 00:10:10,713
front, and the pressure tells the airplane

269
00:10:10,713 --> 00:10:12,745
how fast it is moving through the

270
00:10:12,745 --> 00:10:12,992
air.

271
00:10:13,172 --> 00:10:15,092
If ice blocks the hole,

272
00:10:15,092 --> 00:10:18,013
the airspeed reading becomes wrong.

273
00:10:18,193 --> 00:10:20,845
So the tubes are heated electrically,

274
00:10:20,845 --> 00:10:23,426
sometimes so hot you could burn your

275
00:10:23,426 --> 00:10:24,787
hand on the ground.

276
00:10:25,137 --> 00:10:27,478
On the first of June two thousand

277
00:10:27,478 --> 00:10:30,244
nine, Air France flight four four seven

278
00:10:30,244 --> 00:10:32,940
flew through a storm over the Atlantic

279
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:33,578
at night.

280
00:10:33,758 --> 00:10:36,560
Ice crystals blocked the pitot tubes for

281
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:37,891
less than a minute.

282
00:10:38,071 --> 00:10:40,257
The autopilot disconnected,

283
00:10:40,257 --> 00:10:43,010
the crew was confused by the wrong

284
00:10:43,010 --> 00:10:46,330
speeds, and the airplane stalled and fell

285
00:10:46,330 --> 00:10:47,545
into the ocean.

286
00:10:47,725 --> 00:10:50,209
Two hundred twenty-eight people died.

287
00:10:50,389 --> 00:10:52,764
The tubes on that type of airplane

288
00:10:52,764 --> 00:10:55,068
were replaced after the accident.

289
00:10:55,418 --> 00:10:58,354
Airplanes have several pitot tubes,

290
00:10:58,354 --> 00:11:01,289
so the systems can compare them and

291
00:11:01,289 --> 00:11:02,547
spot a bad one.

292
00:11:02,727 --> 00:11:03,796
On the ground,

293
00:11:03,796 --> 00:11:06,697
they are often covered with red covers

294
00:11:06,697 --> 00:11:08,988
that say remove before flight,

295
00:11:08,988 --> 00:11:11,202
to keep insects and dirt out.

296
00:11:11,382 --> 00:11:13,295
A tiny tube on the nose,

297
00:11:13,295 --> 00:11:16,084
and a whole airliner depends on it.

298
00:11:16,434 --> 00:11:18,150
Why is there a light on the

299
00:11:18,150 --> 00:11:20,310
side of an airplane that shines on

300
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:21,137
its own wing?

301
00:11:21,317 --> 00:11:23,639
It is the wing inspection light.

302
00:11:23,819 --> 00:11:26,358
At night, the pilots and the cabin

303
00:11:26,358 --> 00:11:28,524
crew can switch it on to look

304
00:11:28,524 --> 00:11:30,839
at the leading edge of the wing

305
00:11:30,839 --> 00:11:32,034
and the engines,

306
00:11:32,034 --> 00:11:33,304
to check for ice.

307
00:11:33,484 --> 00:11:35,013
Without it, at night,

308
00:11:35,013 --> 00:11:37,999
nobody could see whether ice was building

309
00:11:37,999 --> 00:11:39,091
up on the wing.

310
00:11:39,441 --> 00:11:40,893
It sounds simple,

311
00:11:40,893 --> 00:11:42,089
because it is.

312
00:11:42,269 --> 00:11:44,736
Even with sensors and heaters,

313
00:11:44,736 --> 00:11:47,121
aviation likes a human check.

314
00:11:47,301 --> 00:11:49,124
Before takeoff in winter,

315
00:11:49,124 --> 00:11:51,530
crews often look at the wing from

316
00:11:51,530 --> 00:11:52,259
the cabin.

317
00:11:52,439 --> 00:11:54,686
If a passenger sees snow on the

318
00:11:54,686 --> 00:11:56,136
wing after de-icing,

319
00:11:56,136 --> 00:11:57,803
the crew wants to know.

320
00:11:57,983 --> 00:12:00,199
A light and a pair of eyes

321
00:12:00,199 --> 00:12:01,988
can stop an accident.

322
00:12:02,338 --> 00:12:05,642
Many passengers notice this light at night

323
00:12:05,642 --> 00:12:07,923
and think something is wrong.

324
00:12:08,103 --> 00:12:10,423
It is just the airplane looking at

325
00:12:10,423 --> 00:12:10,901
itself.

326
00:12:11,081 --> 00:12:12,663
It is one of the few parts

327
00:12:12,663 --> 00:12:14,731
on this list that you can actually

328
00:12:14,731 --> 00:12:15,887
see from your seat.

329
00:12:16,237 --> 00:12:18,170
How does an airplane know it is

330
00:12:18,170 --> 00:12:19,105
collecting ice?

331
00:12:19,285 --> 00:12:22,217
Many airliners have a small probe sticking

332
00:12:22,217 --> 00:12:23,683
out into the airflow,

333
00:12:23,683 --> 00:12:24,870
the ice detector.

334
00:12:25,050 --> 00:12:28,247
Inside, a little rod vibrates at a

335
00:12:28,247 --> 00:12:29,752
fixed frequency.

336
00:12:29,932 --> 00:12:31,926
When ice builds up on it,

337
00:12:31,926 --> 00:12:34,958
the extra weight makes it vibrate more

338
00:12:34,958 --> 00:12:35,516
slowly.

339
00:12:35,696 --> 00:12:38,138
The system notices the change,

340
00:12:38,138 --> 00:12:39,521
warns the pilots,

341
00:12:39,521 --> 00:12:42,777
and on some airplanes turns the anti-ice

342
00:12:42,777 --> 00:12:43,835
on by itself.

343
00:12:44,184 --> 00:12:45,858
After each detection,

344
00:12:45,858 --> 00:12:48,567
the probe heats itself to melt its

345
00:12:48,567 --> 00:12:51,754
own ice, and then starts counting again.

346
00:12:51,934 --> 00:12:54,088
That way it can keep telling the

347
00:12:54,088 --> 00:12:56,174
crew if they are still in icing

348
00:12:56,174 --> 00:12:56,915
conditions.

349
00:12:57,095 --> 00:12:58,571
It is a tiny scale,

350
00:12:58,571 --> 00:13:00,591
weighing ice in real time,

351
00:13:00,591 --> 00:13:03,699
hundreds of times faster than any person

352
00:13:03,699 --> 00:13:04,165
could.

353
00:13:04,515 --> 00:13:06,998
From the orange truck at the gate

354
00:13:06,998 --> 00:13:09,631
to the vibrating probe on the nose,

355
00:13:09,631 --> 00:13:11,888
all ten parts do the same job.

356
00:13:12,068 --> 00:13:15,124
They keep the airplane exactly the shape

357
00:13:15,124 --> 00:13:16,805
it was designed to be.

358
00:13:16,985 --> 00:13:19,633
Because a wing does not care how

359
00:13:19,633 --> 00:13:22,116
big or modern the airplane is.

360
00:13:22,296 --> 00:13:23,422
With ice on it,

361
00:13:23,422 --> 00:13:25,222
it is just a worse wing.

362
00:13:25,571 --> 00:13:27,081
So here are the ten.

363
00:13:27,261 --> 00:13:29,667
Orange fluid that cleans,

364
00:13:29,667 --> 00:13:32,555
and green fluid that protects.

365
00:13:32,735 --> 00:13:35,125
A clean wing rule written by a

366
00:13:35,125 --> 00:13:37,675
crash, and a special pad next to

367
00:13:37,675 --> 00:13:38,552
the runway.

368
00:13:38,732 --> 00:13:39,935
Hot wing edges,

369
00:13:39,935 --> 00:13:41,219
hot engine lips,

370
00:13:41,219 --> 00:13:43,788
rubber boots that break the ice,

371
00:13:43,788 --> 00:13:45,874
heated tubes for airspeed,

372
00:13:45,874 --> 00:13:48,442
a light that shines on the wing,

373
00:13:48,442 --> 00:13:50,850
and a probe that weighs ice as

374
00:13:50,850 --> 00:13:51,572
it forms.

375
00:13:51,922 --> 00:13:53,977
Next time you sit through de-icing,

376
00:13:53,977 --> 00:13:55,678
you will know why it is worth

377
00:13:55,678 --> 00:13:56,207
the wait.

378
00:13:56,387 --> 00:13:59,441
If you want more machines explained part

379
00:13:59,441 --> 00:14:02,342
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

380
00:14:02,522 --> 00:14:04,460
And tell us in the comments which

381
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:06,397
machine you want us to take apart

382
00:14:06,397 --> 00:14:06,690
next.
